WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
- And I'm so excited y'all are here.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.990
But two years ago you
changed your band name.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.390
And it took me a minute, y'all,

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.331
'cuz I was like, so used
to it from childhood.

00:00:08.331 --> 00:00:09.164
- [Band Member] Yeah.

00:00:09.164 --> 00:00:11.340
- But then I heard that this
was something that y'all

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.860
kind of had in the making
for a while, right?

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:14.693
- Yeah.

00:00:14.693 --> 00:00:15.630
- What was so important to you

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.000
to change it to just The Chicks?

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.570
- Well, I think just after
George Floyd and all of that,

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.150
we'd wanted to get rid of
the Dixie part of our name

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.581
for a long time, but it seemed
like a huge thing to do.

00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:30.990
So we would call, you know,
our merch would say DCX,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.360
and we always referred to
ourselves as The Chicks.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.780
So it seemed like a really natural change

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.430
and it seemed kind of seamless.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.350
- Here's what was cool is you, like,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.730
educated a lot of us as well

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.790
because I had no idea
that it meant anything.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.220
So like I had to, like, Goo,
I was like, "Wait, what?"

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.770
And I was like Googling and it
was interesting to find out,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.781
you know, kind of the origin of that word.

00:00:51.781 --> 00:00:53.640
- Yeah, I did the same, I
always thought it was a region.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.870
Like if it's what, what direction?

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.341
- South of the Mason-Dixon Line.

00:00:57.341 --> 00:00:58.330
(laughing)

00:00:58.330 --> 00:00:59.579
- Yeah. That's me too. So it was like,

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:01.080
it was interesting though.
I thought that that was,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.600
that was a really cool to
educate the public, though,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.760
on something, especially
at such an important time.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:06.593
- [Natalie] Yeah.

00:01:06.593 --> 00:01:07.680
- So I thought it was really
cool that y'all did that.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.530
And also it's just, like, easier to say.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:10.363
- (giggles)

00:01:10.363 --> 00:01:12.272
- [Kelly] So that's awesome. I'm lazy.

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:13.230
- (laughs)

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.480
- But I love, and then I think everybody

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.100
would always say that, too,
"Oh, did you see The Chicks?

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:17.933
You see the concert?"

00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:18.766
- Right

00:01:18.766 --> 00:01:19.599
- Everybody would always call y'all that.

00:01:19.599 --> 00:01:20.432
- Yeah.

00:01:20.432 --> 00:01:21.510
- I think super fans like me.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.950
So, what do you each remember

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.070
from like the very first time
that y'all played together?

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.680
Was it like awkward, or
like, you knew right away

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.090
it was gonna work.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.480
- Well, Natalie had joined
the band, a previous lineup,

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.300
and so Marty and I were
very, like, nervous,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.130
Like, "Will she, rehearse all the songs

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:38.963
and know all the songs?"

00:01:38.963 --> 00:01:41.131
Natalie was just like, "Oh, I
got this, y'all, I got this."

00:01:41.131 --> 00:01:42.040
- (laughing)

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:42.873
- [Emily] She had this confidence,

00:01:42.873 --> 00:01:44.760
and Marty and I are just like,
"Oh my gosh, oh my gosh!"

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.250
But Natalie brought this humongous pad

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.280
with her lyrics on it,

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.113
(giggles)

00:01:49.113 --> 00:01:50.940
and we just flip it in between songs.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.280
- We played at these two
clubs in, outside of Houston.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.113
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:55.026
- And I would just

00:01:55.026 --> 00:01:56.760
(all laugh)

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.461
bend down, turn the page.

00:01:58.461 --> 00:01:59.294
(laughing)

00:01:59.294 --> 00:02:00.900
- Oh my God, do you have a
problem with lyrics? I do.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.260
- No, but I had to learn
22 songs in seven days.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.593
I would just listen with
headphones and walk around.

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:07.950
- And you did your yodel.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.352
- Yeah, I had never yodeled before.

00:02:10.352 --> 00:02:11.460
(laughing)

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.360
- That's a lot.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.350
- [Natalie] I know.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.472
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:02:14.472 --> 00:02:15.305
(laughing)

00:02:15.305 --> 00:02:17.550
So the band was famously, I
had to be reminded of this,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.770
The band was famously
boycotted on country radio

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:24.060
19 years ago, which feels
like forever ago to me.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.250
But it happened after,
Natalie, you spoke out

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.490
against the war in Iraq and
the president in 2003, right?

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.500
So what did you, I ended up,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:32.610
it was Shut Up And Sing, right?

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:33.443
The documentary that came out?

00:02:33.443 --> 00:02:34.346
- Yeah.

00:02:34.346 --> 00:02:35.259
- [Kelly] I loved that.

00:02:35.259 --> 00:02:36.092
- (chuckles)

00:02:36.092 --> 00:02:37.350
- I think a lot of
artists or anybody that's

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:41.040
under a microscope, I'm
jumping straight to that,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.690
I'm gonna ask the question, but
I'm jumping straight to that

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.720
because when you're under a
microscope, everything you say,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.000
even if you don't mean something
some way, or even if you,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.490
it's in a moment, everything
gets blown up, good or bad.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.350
And it's a very hard way to live.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.940
And especially being in an industry,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.530
like, I've been told to shut up and sing

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:56.550
and not have an opinion.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.660
And it's very hard, 'cuz you're
a human and we all count,

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.730
you know, our thoughts
and our hearts count.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.730
So how did y'all deal with that?

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.340
That had to be hard after
watching that, Shut Up And Sing,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.930
that was hard, not only on y'all

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.330
but the people around you, right?

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.100
- Yeah, I don't know, I'm a fighter,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.411
so I didn't, like, crumble,

00:03:16.411 --> 00:03:17.244
(laughing)

00:03:17.244 --> 00:03:18.164
I crumbled,

00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:19.140
(audience applause)

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.713
- That's a Texas woman.

00:03:20.713 --> 00:03:24.270
I did crumble, like, after
we won all the Grammys,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.650
I kind of fell apart after that.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.420
I think I was just,
like, in this fight mode,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.400
and then it all sort
of came crashing down.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.500
- Yeah. It's a lot of
weight to carry, yeah.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.142
- Yeah. And it was for how it
was affecting other people,

00:03:33.142 --> 00:03:34.510
I didn't care how it was affecting me.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:35.406
- [Kelly] That's what I'm saying, like,

00:03:35.406 --> 00:03:36.513
- But yeah.

